import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/urls';
---

<Page
  article
  title="Not following back vs. unfollowed: what a report proves"
  description="A current follow-back report cannot prove a historical unfollow. Learn how to interpret qualified differences between compatible snapshots."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE · READING RESULTS</p><h1>
    Not following back isn’t the same as unfollowed.
  </h1><p class="lede">
    One describes a relationship in supplied data. The other describes a change
    over time. A single report cannot bridge that gap.
  </p>
  <h2>One report describes a present comparison</h2><p>
    If an account is in your following list and absent from your supplied
    followers list, it belongs in “not following you back” for that source. It
    may never have followed you. That label does not say whether a change
    happened or when.
  </p>
  <h2>Two snapshots can reveal a difference</h2><p>
    Suppose a fictional account called ada appears in an earlier complete
    followers snapshot and is absent from a later compatible snapshot. The
    careful description is: <strong
      >present in the earlier snapshot; absent in the later snapshot</strong
    >.
  </p><p>
    That observation is stronger than a single current report, but it still does
    not establish an exact unfollow time, a reason, or intent. Username changes,
    deactivation, source behavior, and collection-window differences can
    complicate interpretation.
  </p>
  <h2>Compatible inputs come first</h2><ul>
    <li>
      Both snapshots must describe the same account. Stable IDs help where the
      source provides them.
    </li><li>
      Both need compatible sources and comparison scopes; known upload omissions
      remain qualifications. Automatic-source snapshots require source
      completeness.
    </li><li>
      The source collection dates must be known and ordered. A file’s import or
      save date does not establish when its contents were collected.
    </li><li>
      If identity is based only on usernames, rename and reactivation
      uncertainty remains.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Local snapshots are optional</h2><p>
    MutualLens saves a snapshot only when you choose to do so. It stays on the
    checker’s browser origin. This is convenient for comparing your own records,
    but anyone using the same browser profile may be able to view those saved
    lists. Export a snapshot if you need your own backup, and delete local
    copies when appropriate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href={sitePath('/guides/reading-snapshot-history/')}
      >Read the snapshot-history guide</a
    >, <a href={sitePath('/privacy/')}>review the storage details</a>, or return
    to
    <a href={sitePath('/guides/followers-vs-following/')}
      >followers vs. following</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p><a href={sitePath('/guides/')}>Browse all MutualLens guides</a>.</p>
</Page>
